{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested at exact 8cf82e8d429e351f39662f9480fa5a992c9a9313 (branch\nrequest/projection-cost), reviewed against the conditions of the governing\nrequest git:sha1…5616ac4. The performance win is real, independently\nre-derived, and the reasoning behind the memos is sound. One condition of\nsatisfaction is not met, and the remedy simplifies the head rather than\nenlarging it.\n\nWHAT I RAN, AND WHAT I DID NOT\n\nRan, in /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-projection-cost at this exact head:\ngo test ./... -timeout 40m -\u003e exit 0, 37 packages, no failures (all workroom\nand docset results were already cached from the implementer's run, so I also\nran go test -count=1 ./internal/workroom/ -\u003e exit 0, 6.45 s, and go test -race\n-count=1 ./internal/workroom/ -\u003e exit 0, 8.69 s). go vet ./... -\u003e exit 0.\nmake docs is go test ./internal/docset/..., which that run covers.\n\nAlso ran my own differential, which is the evidence I am resting the\narchitecture conclusion on. I extracted the head's parent tree (ccd161c6, which\nis main, so there is no drift) to a scratch directory, put the same generated\ncorpus and the same projection dumper into both copies as a test file, and\ncompared the JSON of Projection plus Vocabulary byte for byte. The corpus is 12\nmerge rounds: receipts declaring both a directory successor and a nested\ndirectory successor, artifacts in the receipt-to-successor interval, left-live\nsiblings on alternate rounds, condemning retirements with no successor,\nsuccession debt, and a promise retirement placed before each receipt so the\nreceipt is itself stale and its successors exercise the checkpoint. Result:\n183,168 bytes, byte-identical between ccd161c6 and 8cf82e8d. I instrumented the\nhead copy to confirm the corpus reaches the code under review rather than\npassing by it: receiptCheckpointSettles answered settled 78 times with 36 memo\nhits, 30 artifacts project stale, 12 carry describes_superseded_world with a\nworld_superseded_at, 18 carry succession_unrecorded, omitted_supersessions is\n24. I then disabled the memo in the head copy and re-dumped: memo-on and\nmemo-off are byte-identical on that corpus, which is the direct soundness test\nfor part 4 rather than an argument about it.\n\nDid not re-derive the report's two real-log numbers (18.10 s -\u003e 2.11 s on the\nartifact-heavy workroom, 21.70 s -\u003e 13.32 s on this repository) or its\nbyte-identity claims on those two logs. I take the shape of the claim on the\nstrength of my own differential, and the timings on the report's word.\n\n1. ARCHITECTURE\n\nAffected layers of docs/reference/architecture.md: layer 5, Application profile\nand interpreter, whose Workroom fold this is; and layer 6, Projections and\nqueries, whose read model project() builds. Layer 4's application host binding\nis touched indirectly and only through one sentence of its contract, the one\nthat matters here: internal/app \"gates its separate projection cache on the\nselected application and fold version\". Layer 5 states that rule the other way\nround throughout — a change that reinterprets records already in the log, or\nthat changes the exact projection bytes a cache is keyed on, must advance\nworkroom-fold@N, and a cache written under the old number is rejected and\nverified history replayed.\n\nSo the architecture question for this head is not whether the page mentions\npathCovers or postReceiptAccounting; it mentions neither, and nothing in layer\n6's owned surfaces (statusview, the until filter, WorkOf, the artifact page,\nthe review gate) changes shape. The question is whether the projection bytes\nmove, because if they do, this head owes a profile bump and a page edit, and if\nthey do not, it owes neither. I verified they do not, on a corpus built to make\nthem move: same bytes across the parent and this head, through the checkpoint,\nthe interval accounting, the sibling protection, the world flag and the\nsuccession counts. No new field, no renamed field, no reordered slice. The fold\nprofile is correctly left alone.\n\nI checked the two structural invariants the new indexes stand on rather than\ntaking them from the comments. Folder.Append calls only state.append and never\nprojects, and project() is reached only from Projection(), Fold and Evaluate,\nso retired() really is final for the length of one projection and caching an\nartifact's retired flag inside receiptScan is sound. The only new field on\nfoldState is supersessionsByTarget, written in append beside f.supersessions in\nthe same *Supersede case and nowhere else; everything else the change adds\nlives on the stack of one project() or one computeStaleness. That means no new\nshared mutable state, which is why -race stays clean and why two callers folding\nthe same Folder concurrently are no worse off than before.\n\nConclusion: layers 5 and 6 are affected, this head preserves their contract,\nand docs/reference/architecture.md correctly does not move. I do not request\nchanges on this ground.\n\n2. SECURITY\n\nI read each boundary the change sits on and could not find a way for one\nactor's claim to reach an answer it could not reach before, or for an answer\ncomputed under an older world to survive the world moving. In detail:\n\nMerge-receipt authority and the sealed plan. receiptCheckpointSettles keeps\nevery gate in the order it had: prospective-receipt seam (mergeLeftLivePresent\nand mergeChangedPathsValid, both halves), a non-nil sealed mergePlan, the\nsuccessor signed by the receipt's own author, and publishedByMerge. The memo\nlookup is placed after all four, so a bystander that merely cites the receipt\nstill cannot borrow a memoized answer — which was the one way a memo here could\nhave become an authority leak, and it is not open.\n\nLeft-live testimony written by the merge's own signer. Still not read for\nfreshness. hasPostReceiptLiveArtifact now reads a per-exact-path index instead\nof scanning to the end of the log, and the index is keyed on the artifact's\npath only, so nothing receipt-specific or actor-specific is shared between\nreceipts; the post-frontier bound and the exclusion of the receipt's own\nsuccessor are applied per call, at read time. Mutating away either of those two\nturns three named tests red, so the narrow scope of a receipt's protection is\nstill pinned.\n\nStaleness propagation and the per-receipt memo. The soundness argument holds,\nand I checked its two premises in the code rather than in the comment.\nstalenessScope.closure sorts its indexes, so computeStaleness visits records in\nascending index order, and stale[id] is written only in that record's own\niteration, so staleness for every index below the cursor is final. The kernel\nrefuses a rests_on entry naming an in-log event the log does not already hold\n(internal/kernel/kernel.go, resolveReferences with inLogEvent), so a basis\nalways has a lower index than its dependent, so a receipt's whole basis closure\nlies at or below the receipt's index, and a successor is always above it. By\nthe time any successor is projected, every staleness value the walk reads is\nfinal. The other inputs the memo key omits are constant per receipt within one\npass: plan is receipt.mergePlan narrowed by withoutCondemnedSuccessions, which\nreads only successors and retired() and never stale; successors is a pass\nparameter; s.active and s.retired are scope state. The memo key — the receipt\nevent — is therefore complete. My memo-on/memo-off byte comparison on a corpus\nwhere the checkpoint fires 78 times agrees.\n\nThe resumed cursor and the empty-interval fast path. The report and the code\ncomment both say these assume project() asks about one receipt's successors in\nascending index order. They do not, and that is the better news: upTo only ever\nadvances (for ; scan.cursor \u003c limit; scan.cursor++, so a smaller limit is a\nno-op), byPath buckets are appended in cursor order and so are ascending, and\npostReceiptAccounting bounds every answer with its own break at covered.index\n\u003e= successor.index. Descending and repeated questions therefore give the same\nanswers, which is what TestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessorAndRepeats asks on\npurpose. Order is a performance assumption only; nothing breaks if it changes.\nI would fix the two comments to say that, since as written they overstate a\ndependency the code does not have and invite a future reader to add a guard for\nit.\n\nFacts cached in receiptScan. index, event and retired are all final for the\nprojection that caches them, per the Append/project separation above.\nTestProjectionIndexesDoNotOutliveTheirFold pins the live half against an append\nafter a projection, and mutating retired to a constant false turns that test\nand TestPostReceiptAccountingSeparatesCountedFromOwed red.\n\npathCovers. I checked the rewrite for equivalence rather than for plausibility,\nincluding the cases the test table does not name: successor \"/\" (directory \"\"\nand predecessor[0] == '/' reproduces HasPrefix(predecessor, \"/\")), successor\n\"a//\" (TrimSuffix removes one slash, so the compared prefix is \"a/\" and the\nseparator byte is the second slash, matching HasPrefix(predecessor, \"a//\")),\nand predecessor exactly equal to the trimmed directory (both refuse). The\nlength test is strict, so the index read cannot go out of range.\n\nSuccession debt. postReceiptAccounting's frozen count and live subset come from\none walk instead of two, and the subtraction of accountedDeletions is\nuntouched. Widening the empty-interval fast path by one turns five tests red.\n\nNo unresolved security defect. I do not request changes on this ground.\n\n3. SIMPLIFICATION — and the reason for changes-requested\n\nCondition 5 of the governing request is \"Named unit tests cover each optimized\npath, and a mutation of each new guard is shown to turn a test red.\" This head\ndoes not meet it. The report says so for one guard, and I found the same true\nof two more. I chose my own mutations, applied them to a copy, and restored the\nfile each time; the worktree is unmodified and at 8cf82e8d.\n\nKilled, as claimed (9): pathCovers with the separator byte check dropped -\u003e\nTestSuccessionFollowsOnlyACoveringSuccessor/prefix_that_is_not_a_directory and\nTestPathCoversBoundariesWithoutAllocating. pathCovers with the strict length\ntest relaxed to \u003e= -\u003e TestMergeLeftLiveUsesClosestCoveringSuccessorForNested-\nArtifacts. The per-successor break removed -\u003e\nTestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessorAndRepeats. Empty-interval fast path\nwidened by one -\u003e five tests. receiptScan caching retired as false -\u003e two.\nsupersessionsByTarget keyed on the supersession instead of its target -\u003e eleven,\nincluding the canonical rejected-round migration fixture. The post-frontier\nbound in hasPostReceiptLiveArtifact removed -\u003e three. The forward notice and the\nmemo gate removed together -\u003e TestReceiptWalkNoticesAForwardCitation. The\nsibling protection's unsettled-commitment gate replaced by true -\u003e three.\n\nSurvived (4), and these are the findings.\n\n(a) The forward-citation notice does not cover the read the memo's soundness\ndepends on, so the guard does not do the job it is kept for. The memo gate\ntrusts w.forward, and w.forward is set in exactly one place, fold.go:3107,\nwhere the walk recurses into a record above the frontier. But fold.go:3114 is\nthe other read of pass-mutable state: the basis loop evaluates w.stale[basis]\nto decide whether to recurse, and when that test says continue the walk has\nread the staleness of a record it never enters. If that basis sits above the\nfrontier its staleness is not yet decided, the read is exactly the unsound one,\nand forward stays false, so the answer is memoized. I proved it directly rather\nthan arguing it: a fold in which \"early\" rests on the later \"late\", walked with\nfrontier = early.index, answers settled with forward false, and the same walk\nwith stale[\"late\"] preset recurses and does notice. So the guard fires only in\nthe case where the unsoundness would have been visible anyway.\n\nThis is not a live defect. resolveReferences makes the whole class unreachable\nin a sequenced log, which is why nothing is broken today and why removing the\ngate leaves the suite green. But the stated reason for keeping it is that the\nmemo \"breaks silently when that layer changes\" — and if that layer changed,\nthis guard would still let the memo break silently, through fold.go:3114. A\nhalf-guard is worse than none: it reads as a proof that is not one, and it is\nthe thing a future reader will trust instead of re-deriving the argument.\n\nMy recommendation is to delete it: the forward field, the fold.go:3107 notice,\nthe fold.go:3043 gate, and TestReceiptWalkNoticesAForwardCitation. Memoize\nunconditionally and state the real argument at the memo, naming\ninternal/kernel/kernel.go resolveReferences as the invariant and the ascending\nclosure as the ordering premise. That is less code, one fewer test, no guard\nwhose mutation survives, and condition 5 met rather than excepted. Completing\nthe guard instead — setting forward on every read of w.stale for a record above\nthe frontier — is also correct and I would accept it, but it buys a defence\nagainst something the kernel forbids, and it still has no red proof, so it does\nnot clear condition 5.\n\n(b) The report's claimed red proof for part 5 does not hold. It lists\nTestMergeChangedPathsProtectsNestedSiblingsUntilNamedCommitmentEnds,\nTestMergeLeftLiveProtectedSiblingIsNotRetirementDebt and\nTestReceiptProtectionBeforeAtAndAfterClosure as the mutation evidence for\n\"protection read with the projection's own staleness\". Replacing result.stale\nat fold.go:3409 with an empty map leaves the entire package green, and so does\ndropping \"stale\" from the active-status switch in unsettledCommitmentsIn. The\nreason is reassuring for safety — that switch admits \"stale\" alongside open,\npromised, reported, awaiting-review, awaiting-authorization and\nawaiting-landing, so which set is passed barely moves the answer — but it means\nthe substitution of result.stale for a second full pass has no red proof, and\nthe report states one it does not have. Either add a fixture where a row's\nstaleness is the difference (a commitment whose only unfinished status is\nstale), or say plainly in the report that this part is proved by the\ndifferential and not by a mutation. I would accept the second; what I will not\napprove is the table as written.\n\n(c) The retired filter in liveArtifactIndex.at, fold.go:3755, has no red proof\neither: removing it leaves the package green. It is a moved guard rather than a\nnew one — main had the same f.retired call inside hasPostReceiptLiveArtifact,\nequally untested — so I am not asking for a fixture. I note it because the\nreport's table reads as complete coverage of the new guards and it is not, and\nbecause a retired later artifact at a sibling's exact path wrongly counted as\nnew unsealed work would silently turn a protected sibling into retirement debt.\n\n(d) Removing the memo lookup altogether leaves the package green. That one is\ncorrect and the report says so: it changes speed, not answers.\n\nOn the new test file pulling its weight: it does, with one exception.\nTestPathCoversBoundariesWithoutAllocating, TestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessor-\nAndRepeats, TestPostReceiptAccountingStopsAtTheSuccessor,\nTestPostReceiptAccountingSeparatesCountedFromOwed,\nTestProjectionIndexesDoNotOutliveTheirFold and\nTestSupersessionIndexHoldsEachTargetInLogOrder each have a mutation only they\ncatch, and the shared costReceipt/costArtifact/costRecords helpers are smaller\nthan the fixtures they replace. TestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessorAndRepeats\nearns its place twice over by asking out of order. The exception is\nTestReceiptWalkNoticesAForwardCitation, which tests the guard in (a) and should\ngo with it. The two benchmarks and artifactHeavyHistory are worth keeping: they\nare the only corpus in the tree with the shape the request was filed about.\n\nWHAT WOULD MAKE THIS APPROVED\n\nEither delete the forward field, its notice, its gate and its test and state the\nkernel invariant at the memo; or complete the guard at fold.go:3114 and say in\nthe report that it has no red proof. And correct the part 5 line of the report's\nmutation table to what is actually demonstrated. Republish the artifact set at\nthe new head — fold.go, projection_cost_test.go and performance.md all move —\nand I will re-review the delta. Nothing in conclusions 1 or 2 needs to change.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:27819f5954ef6c1f4b4da7b2b395b731ac83b29c","binding":"assigned","head":"8cf82e8d429e351f39662f9480fa5a992c9a9313","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:fd9afd9f934a152d15dca6c94d794f67fce71819\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b550f9bba8a3baff29673c3c76f6ffa93bc25db3\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4d0dbdd51efe37b8ce69deb40ea1961d762bf4cf\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b550f9bba8a3baff29673c3c76f6ffa93bc25db3","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}